Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Graph PD Notation with Colored Arcs", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.924093587385165*^9, 3.924093606767956*^9}, {
  3.932737827098742*^9, 3.932737840936195*^9}, {3.932737873495603*^9, 
  3.932737877369068*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b5a5d6eb-8dee-4293-bcdb-\
a4a53aed45d3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We begin with the same graph PD notation as before (see the file \
ProofCode-6CapGenerator.nb), with a modification that allows a color to be \
assigned to the arc.\n\nEach arc[a,b] is replaced with arc[c, A[a,b]] where \
A[a,b] tracks the original arc information, and c gives the current color of \
the arc.  The colors are represented by a number, 1, 2, 3, or 4, thought of \
as red, blue, green, and yellow, respectively.  An arc labeled with 0 is \
treated as an uncolored arc.  \n\nIn the code below we generate all possible \
3-colorings (using colors 1, 2, and 3) of the planar six caps that can be \
used to color our reducer (shown below).  One simplification is made in that \
we assume arcs one and two (as named by the smallest arc-label) are always \
colored red and blue, respectively.  Any other coloring of the cap that \
colors these two arcs differently will differ from one of the included \
colorings by an overall permutation of the colors.  The results are exported \
to an .mx file saved in the user\[CloseCurlyQuote]s default directory.\n\n\
Reducer:\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  GraphicsBox[
   TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["
